{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1488357-divinity-dice-a-bit-excessive","id":1488357,"title":"Divinity dice: a bit excessive?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-07-29T07:36:19-07:00","id":1488357,"title":"Divinity dice: a bit excessive?","content":"So, I've been reading the Demigod book. There's a lot of new material to take in, but the thing that really caught my eye is divinity dice.<br /> <br /> Unless I'm misunderstanding the rules, it seems like once someone has gained even a small number of divinity dice, they're going to be rolling catastrophic successes <i>a lot</i> and mortal failures uncomfortably often. And it doesn't seem like there's much they can actually do to mitigate that risk, short of 1) flooding the pool somewhere they don't care about collateral damage (which you can do only once in a session) or 2) just not doing any of the things that give you divinity dice (but then why are you playing a Demigod game at all?).<br /> <br /> It would have made sense and been cool to have &quot;flooding the pool&quot; as a high-risk/high-reward thing -- in a crisis, you draw on extra divine power and hope like hell you can control the fallout. That's cool. But the way this is written, once you've picked up a couple of divinity dice, you're rolling them on every Skill roll no matter what.<br /> <br /> On a related note, I was a bit confused to discover that the Collateral Pool in Aberrant 2e actually has a &quot;Controlled Chaos&quot; stunt so that nova PCs can work to reduce the collateral damage of their antics, but I didn't see anything equivalent in any of the Scion books. This is especially worrisome because <i>rolling a pool with divinity dice</i> is on the list of things that add to the Collateral Pool.<br /> <br /> It seems like demigods are destined to cause havoc anywhere they go. Am I missing something here? I thought there were enough mechanics to keep a Scion's life &quot;interesting&quot; already. &gt;_&gt;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11237-jefepato","name":"Jefepato","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11237_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-29T09:16:08-07:00","id":1488367,"title":"","content":"I think the point of Divinity Dice is, in part, to set the stage for <b>Scion: God</b>: the Catastrophic Successes and Mortal Failures are indicative of the issues that Gods face that tend to cause them to use agents in the World instead of involving themselves directly. <br /> <br /> That said, you may have a point in how severe it is. I wouldn't want to make it strictly an opt-in thing; but I could see allowing the Scion some sort of opt-out: say, spend a point of Legend to suppress your Divinity Dice for a scene. Maybe even adjust Casual Miracles to serve as a way to bleed off excess Divinity Dice: whenever you perform a Casual Miracle, you get the option to roll your Divinity Dice, removing any successes from the pool but inviting a Mortal Failure if you don't get any successes. The idea is that you have to <i>use</i> your Divinity Dice in some way in order to get rid of them; but that use doesn't necessarily have to involve Catastrophic Success or increases to the Collateral pool.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-29T15:42:07-07:00","id":1488399,"title":"","content":"Having a way to &quot;bleed off&quot; excess divinity dice at all would make a huge difference. As things are, someone could be walking around with two divinity dice (you need three before you can flood the pool) and dealing with an endless series of catastrophic successes all day long, even if they don't attempt any further supernatural actions.<br /> <br /> Honestly, it just doesn't make any sense to me that a Demigod can have enough control of their power to do casual miracles with no trouble*, but be inept enough with their power to accidentally cut loose <i>when they weren't even trying to use it at all</i>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\">*aside from the risk of Fatebinding, but Fate is going to mess with a Scion's life no matter what</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11237-jefepato","name":"Jefepato","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11237_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-03T08:41:33-07:00","id":1488977,"title":"","content":"In all honesty, what I would have done is have so that you need a certain amount of successes with Divinity Dice before a Catastrophic Success occurs, rather than just one. Maybe have a sliding scale based on legend?<br /> <br /> Legend 5: 4 successes<br /> Legend 6: 3 successes<br /> Legend 7: 2 successes<br /> Legend 8: 1 success<br /> <br /> That way, the closer you get to Apotheosis, the more difficult it is to reign in your power and thus prevent a Catastrophic success from occurring. Similar thing with Mortal failures, where at Legend 5 you need four divinity dice failing to even achieve that outcome, while at Legend 8 just the 1 is enough to cause massive issues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26317-critian-caceorte","name":"Critian Caceorte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}